Along $OX$, move $2$ squares right and $4$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square left and $2$ squares down. Thus $OX=2|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=-2\mathbf{a}$.

Along $XC$, move $2$ squares right and $2$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares left and $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=1|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=-\mathbf{b}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=-2\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=-2$ and $\mu=-1$.

